From: pasichdev <apasichnik9@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 19:37:04 +0300
Subject: [RFC PATCH net-next 0/2] wireguard: crypto in NAPI on single-CPU systems

On a single-CPU system WireGuard's crypt workqueue cannot add parallelism,
but every packet still pays for its hand-offs: two workqueue hops on
transmit and one on receive, each with a context switch. Single-core MIPS
routers are a common OpenWrt target; on one of them (Realtek RTL8196E,
Lexra RLX4181 at about 400 MHz) WireGuard ran at about 9 Mbit/s while
plain TCP to the same board ran at 73-88 Mbit/s, and the kernel PC
profiler put about 22% of CPU time on context switches.

Patch 2 moves crypto into NAPI polls when num_possible_cpus() == 1: a new
per-peer TX NAPI encrypts and sends, and the existing per-peer NAPI also
decrypts. The per-peer queues and their reference handling stay as they
are. Both polls are budgeted. A sending task runs one budget of the TX
NAPI itself, so a local sender pays for its own encryption; a transmit
nested inside, as in a routing loop, finds the NAPI owned and only queues,
so encryption cannot recurse. Machines with more than one possible CPU
keep the workqueue path.

Patch 1 is a fix found while reviewing that path: packets left on the
per-peer queues when a peer is removed keep the peer alive. It applies on
its own and is reproduced on net-next: removing a peer under a UDP flood
leaked it 5 times in 2140 removals without the fix and 0 times with it.

On that board patch 2 doubles TCP receive throughput through the tunnel
(9.4 to 18.8 Mbit/s), raises send from 8.2 to 13.8 Mbit/s and cuts
in-tunnel ping under load from 108 to 31 ms; the table is in the commit
message. An earlier version that encrypted inline in ndo_start_xmit was
as fast, but allowed recursion through a routing loop and unbounded
bottom-half-off work. On an x86 guest with one vCPU, TCP between two
namespaces gains 7-8%; with four vCPUs nothing changes.

One behaviour change to look at: on one CPU, a UDP flood between two
namespaces now loses about half the packets, like plain UDP over veth on
the same guest, where it lost almost none before because the crypt
workqueue throttled the sender. Numbers are in patch 2.

Related work: Barone et al., "Achieving Linear CPU Scaling in WireGuard
with an Efficient Multi-tunnel Architecture" (netdevconf 0x18), process
WireGuard stages inline per flow to scale over many cores. This series is
the much narrower single-CPU case.

Open questions:

- Is a static key on num_possible_cpus() == 1 the right condition, or
  would you rather have a module parameter or a per-device setting?
- A sending task takes ownership of the TX NAPI with napi_schedule_prep(),
  runs one turn of it and releases it with napi_complete_done(), outside
  a NAPI poll. That serialises it against the poll and against nested
  transmits without a new lock, but it uses NAPI ownership as a
  serialisation primitive from process context, which is unusual. The
  alternative is a per-peer bit taken with test_and_set_bit() for the
  sending task's turn, with NAPI used only for deferred work; with one
  CPU and bottom halves off during the turn, the bit only has to exclude
  nesting. Which would you prefer?
- 16 packets per transmit turn and 8 per queue entry were picked for a
  400 MHz CPU. Should they scale with anything?

This is an RFC because the approach is the question.

Tested: on the board above with Linux 6.18.54; with
tools/testing/selftests/wireguard on net-next (x86 KVM) and on 6.18.54
(arm64, emulated), each with 4 CPUs (workqueue path) and 1 CPU (NAPI
path). The leak reproducer for patch 1 is a modified netns.sh, available
on request.

